WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.300
Multi-agent systems.

2
00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:05.010
Splitting work across agents sounds elegant.

3
00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:07.967
But it has a price, and you should be able to name it.

4
00:00:09.167 --> 00:00:13.767
A multi-agent system is several specialized agents passing messages.

5
00:00:14.067 --> 00:00:15.933
A researcher gathers the data.

6
00:00:16.233 --> 00:00:18.000
It writes to a shared scratchpad.

7
00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:21.158
And a writer turns that into the final brief.

8
00:00:21.158 --> 00:00:25.667
Each agent gets a focused prompt, focused tools, and a cleaner context.

9
00:00:26.867 --> 00:00:28.102
Now the reality check.

10
00:00:28.102 --> 00:00:29.900
There are three costs to recite.

11
00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:34.433
First, coordination overhead: the handoff logic and the shared state.

12
00:00:34.733 --> 00:00:36.369
Second, error compounding.

13
00:00:36.369 --> 00:00:39.767
Every hop can garble what the previous agent produced.

14
00:00:40.067 --> 00:00:42.097
Third, token multiplication.

15
00:00:42.097 --> 00:00:45.867
Context gets re-sent for every agent, on every turn.

16
00:00:47.067 --> 00:00:49.067
So here is the professional stance.

17
00:00:49.367 --> 00:00:52.133
Always build the single-agent baseline first.

18
00:00:52.433 --> 00:01:03.600
Reach for multiple agents only when one agent measurably fails, perhaps because one prompt cannot hold two conflicting personas, or one context cannot hold both workloads.

19
00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:07.067
Here is an experiment that keeps you honest.

20
00:01:07.367 --> 00:01:08.234
First, the pair.

21
00:01:08.234 --> 00:01:11.867
The researcher gathers, and the writer drafts from the shared file.

22
00:01:12.167 --> 00:01:14.700
Then the same task given to a single agent.

23
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:18.600
We compare the token counts, and then blind-grade both briefs.

24
00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:23.767
Here is what happened when the Orbital Research Agent was split into three.

25
00:01:24.067 --> 00:01:26.990
The trio cost two point three times the tokens.

26
00:01:26.990 --> 00:01:31.033
The solo brief had equal facts, but it buried the hazard summary.

27
00:01:32.233 --> 00:01:33.400
Here is the verdict.

28
00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:37.167
You split the agent into a fetcher, an analyst, and a writer.

29
00:01:37.467 --> 00:01:39.400
You measure it against the baseline.

30
00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:46.256
So you keep the researcher and writer pair for the weekly report only, and stay single-agent everywhere else.

31
00:01:46.256 --> 00:01:50.467
That measured, partial adoption is the judgment interviewers look for.

32
00:01:51.667 --> 00:01:52.473
Let's recap.

33
00:01:52.473 --> 00:01:56.033
Multi-agent systems are specialists passing messages.

34
00:01:56.333 --> 00:02:00.067
They cost coordination, compounding errors, and extra tokens.

35
00:02:00.367 --> 00:02:03.900
Build the baseline first, and split only on a measured failure.

36
00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:06.723
Then adopt it only where it pays off.

37
00:02:06.723 --> 00:02:08.700
Next up: agent orchestration.
